/-!
# The radical of the conjugacy equation `z⁻¹ c z d = 1`

Let `G` be a group and `c, d ∈ G`. For `c, d ≠ 1` the one-variable equation
`c^z d = z⁻¹ c z d = 1` is the standard quadratic equation of type (2) with genus `0` and one
conjugated coefficient. If it has a solution `z ↦ b` in `G`, then `d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b`, and `z ↦ g` is
a solution exactly when `g b⁻¹` centralizes `c`. In a CSA group with the big powers property,
for instance a fully residually free group, and for `c ≠ 1`, the radical of the equation is the
normal closure of the commutators `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆` with `h` running through the full centralizer
`C_G(c)`:

* the centralizer of `c ≠ 1` is abelian, so a solution `g` makes `g b⁻¹` commute with every
  `h ∈ C_G(c)`, and the equation is equivalent to the system `[z b⁻¹, C_G(c)] = 1`;
* modulo this system, `t = z b⁻¹` commutes with `C_G(c)`, so `t ↦ z b⁻¹` is a left inverse of
  `z ↦ t b` and the group of the system embeds over `G` in the extension `G(c, ℤ)` of the
  centralizer of `c`. This extension is discriminated, hence separated, by `G`
  (`TauCeti.LimitGroup.CentralizerExt.isDiscriminatedBy`), and separation of the group of the
  system is the statement that the radical of the system is its normal closure.

When `C_G(c)` is the cyclic group generated by `c`, the radical is the normal closure of the
equation itself, which is also the normal closure of `[z b⁻¹, c]`, a conjugate of the inverse of
`z⁻¹ c z d`. In general it is not: over `F(a, t)` the radical of `z⁻¹ a² z a⁻² = 1` contains
`⁅z, a⁆`, which is not in the normal closure of `⁅z, a²⁆`
(`commutator_notMem_normalClosure_commutator_sq`). This is why [VAR-I] uses the full
centralizer, whereas the single-relator formula `R(S) = ncl([z b⁻¹, c])` of [IFT], Theorem 2 (3),
needs `C_G(c) = ⟨c⟩`.

The commutators are Mathlib's `⁅g, h⁆ = g h g⁻¹ h⁻¹`. Since `h` runs through a subgroup, their
normal closure is the same as with the convention `[g, h] = g⁻¹ h⁻¹ g h` of [IFT] and [VAR-I]. The
statements are in one variable, as in the sources.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.mem_V_conjugacy_iff`: if `d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b`, the solutions of
  `z⁻¹ c z d = 1` are the `z` with `z b⁻¹ ∈ C(c)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.V_conjugacy_eq_V_commutator_centralizer`: in a
  commutative-transitive group the equation has the same solutions as the system
  `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆ = 1`, `h ∈ C_G(c)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.Rad_commutator_centralizer_eq_normalClosure`: over a CSA group
  with big powers and for `c ≠ 1`, the radical of this system is its normal closure.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure`: the radical of
  `z⁻¹ c z d = 1` is the normal closure of the `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆`, `h ∈ C_G(c)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure_self`: if `C_G(c) = ⟨c⟩`, the
  radical is the normal closure of the equation.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic.Standard.Rad_toWord_orientableCoeff_zero_one`: the same for the
  standard quadratic equation of type (2) with genus `0` and one coefficient.

## References

* [VAR-I] O. Kharlampovich, A. Myasnikov, *Irreducible affine varieties over a free group. I:
  Irreducibility of quadratic equations and Nullstellensatz*, J. Algebra 200 (1998), §3, Form 2,
  and Lemma 15.
* [IFT] O. Kharlampovich, A. Myasnikov, *Implicit function theorem over free groups*, J. Algebra
  290 (2005), §2.7, Theorem 2 (3).
-/

public section

open Monoid.Coprod Subgroup Group
open scoped commutatorElement

namespace TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic

open LimitGroup

variable {G H X : Type*} [Group G] [Group H]

/-- **The solutions of the conjugacy equation.** If `d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b`, then a point `v` of `H`
solves the equation `z⁻¹ c z d = 1` in the variable `z = x` exactly when `v x * b⁻¹` centralizes
`c`. Here `H` is any group receiving the coefficients through `ι`. -/
theorem mem_V_conjugacy_iff {ι : G →* H} {c d b : G} (hb : d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * c * b) {x : X} {v : X → H} :
    v ∈ V ι {(inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d} ↔
      v x * (ι b)⁻¹ ∈ centralizer {ι c} := by
  have hd : d = b⁻¹ * c⁻¹ * b := by rw [← inv_inv d, hb]; group
  have h : (v x)⁻¹ * ι c * v x * ((ι b)⁻¹ * (ι c)⁻¹ * ι b) =
      (v x)⁻¹ * ⁅ι c, v x * (ι b)⁻¹⁆ * (v x)⁻¹⁻¹ := by
    simp only [commutatorElement_def]
    group
  simp only [mem_V, Set.mem_singleton_iff, forall_eq, map_mul, map_inv, eval_inl, eval_inr,
    FreeGroup.lift_apply_of, hd, h, conj_eq_one_iff, commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute,
    mem_centralizer_singleton_iff, ← commute_iff_eq]
  exact Commute.symm_iff

/-- **The conjugacy equation is equivalent to a system of commutators.** In a
commutative-transitive group `H`, if `d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b` and `c` is not sent to `1`, then
`z⁻¹ c z d = 1` has the same solutions as the system `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆ = 1`, `h ∈ C_G(c)`. -/
theorem V_conjugacy_eq_V_commutator_centralizer {ι : G →* H} (hH : IsCommTransitive H) {c d b : G}
    (hc : ι c ≠ 1) (hb : d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * c * b) (x : X) :
    V ι {(inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d} =
      V ι ((fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
        (centralizer {c} : Set G)) := by
  ext v
  simp only [mem_V_conjugacy_iff hb, mem_V, Set.forall_mem_image, SetLike.mem_coe,
    map_commutatorElement, map_mul, map_inv, eval_inl, eval_inr, FreeGroup.lift_apply_of,
    commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute, mem_centralizer_singleton_iff, ← commute_iff_eq]
  exact ⟨fun hv h hh => (isCommTransitive_iff H).1 hH _ _ _ hc hv (hh.symm.map ι),
    fun hv => hv (Commute.refl c)⟩

/-- **The group of the system `[z b⁻¹, C_G(c)] = 1` is residually `G`.** Over a CSA group `G`
with the big powers property and for `c ≠ 1`, the radical of the system `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆ = 1`,
`h ∈ C_G(c)`, in one variable `z`, is its normal closure: the group of the system embeds over `G`
in the extension `G(c, ℤ)` of the centralizer of `c`, by `z ↦ t b`, and `G(c, ℤ)` is separated
by `G`. -/
theorem Rad_commutator_centralizer_eq_normalClosure [Subsingleton X] (hBP : IsBP G) (hG : IsCSA G)
    {c : G} (hc : c ≠ 1) (b : G) (x : X) :
    Rad (X := X) (.id G) ((fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
        (centralizer {c} : Set G)) =
      normalClosure ((fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
        (centralizer {c} : Set G)) := by
  set T : Set (GX G X) := (fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
    (centralizer {c} : Set G)
  -- The point `z ↦ t b` of `G(c, ℤ)` solves the system.
  have hv : (fun _ => CentralizerExt.t 0 * CentralizerExt.of b) ∈
      V (CentralizerExt.of : G →* CentralizerExt c 1) T := by
    refine mem_V.2 ?_
    rintro _ ⟨h, hh, rfl⟩
    simpa [map_commutatorElement, commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute] using
      (CentralizerExt.commute_of_t hh 0).symm
  -- Conversely `t ↦ z b⁻¹` is a homomorphism `G(c, ℤ) → G_T`, a left inverse.
  have hT (h : G) (hh : h ∈ centralizer {c}) (_ : Fin 1) :
      Commute (SystemGroup.coeff T h) ((inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹ : GX G X) : _) := by
    have h1 := (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).2 (subset_normalClosure ⟨h, hh, rfl⟩ :
      ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆ ∈ normalClosure T)
    rw [← QuotientGroup.mk'_apply, map_commutatorElement,
      commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute] at h1
    simpa using h1.symm
  let ψ : CentralizerExt c 1 →* SystemGroup G T := CentralizerExt.lift (SystemGroup.coeff T)
    (fun _ => ((inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹ : GX G X) : _)) (fun _ _ => .refl _) hT
  have hψ : ψ.comp (eval CentralizerExt.of fun _ => CentralizerExt.t 0 * CentralizerExt.of b) =
      QuotientGroup.mk' _ := by
    refine hom_ext (MonoidHom.ext fun g => ?_) (FreeGroup.ext_hom _ _ fun y => ?_)
    · simp [ψ]
    · simp [ψ, Subsingleton.elim y x]
  rw [← SystemGroup.isSeparatedBy_coeff_iff]
  refine (CentralizerExt.isDiscriminatedBy hBP hG hc).isSeparatedBy.of_injective
    (SystemGroup.evalGHom _ hv) (Function.LeftInverse.injective (g := ψ) fun q => ?_)
  obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk_surjective q
  simpa using DFunLike.congr_fun hψ w

/-- **The radical of the conjugacy equation** ([VAR-I], §3, Form 2). Over a CSA group `G` with
the big powers property, for instance a fully residually free group, let `c ≠ 1` and
`d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b`, so that `z ↦ b` solves `z⁻¹ c z d = 1`. The radical of this equation in one
variable `z` is the normal closure of the commutators `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆` with `h` in the full
centralizer `C_G(c)`. -/
theorem Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure [Subsingleton X] (hBP : IsBP G) (hG : IsCSA G) {c d b : G}
    (hc : c ≠ 1) (hb : d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * c * b) (x : X) :
    Rad (.id G) {(inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d} =
      normalClosure ((fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
        (centralizer {c} : Set G)) := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_conjugacy_eq_V_commutator_centralizer hG.isCommTransitive hc hb, ← Rad_def,
    Rad_commutator_centralizer_eq_normalClosure hBP hG hc]

/-- **The single-relator case.** Over a CSA group `G` with the big powers property, if `c ≠ 1`
generates its own centralizer, `C_G(c) = ⟨c⟩` (only `≤` is assumed, the other inclusion being
automatic), and `d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b`, then the radical of the conjugacy equation `z⁻¹ c z d = 1` is
the normal closure of the equation. -/
theorem Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure_self [Subsingleton X] (hBP : IsBP G) (hG : IsCSA G)
    {c d b : G} (hc : c ≠ 1) (hb : d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * c * b) (hC : centralizer {c} ≤ zpowers c) (x : X) :
    Rad (.id G) {(inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d} =
      normalClosure {(inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d} := by
  set S : GX G X := (inr (FreeGroup.of x))⁻¹ * inl c * inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl d
  refine le_antisymm ?_ (normalClosure_le_Rad _)
  rw [Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure hBP hG hc hb]
  refine normalClosure_le_normal ?_
  rintro _ ⟨_, hh, rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := hC hh
  -- The generic point of `G_S` solves `S`, so there `z b⁻¹` commutes with `c`, hence with `c ^ k`.
  have hv := (mem_V_conjugacy_iff hb).1 (SystemGroup.homEquiv _ {S} (GHom.id _)).2
  simp only [SystemGroup.homEquiv_apply_coe, GHom.coe_id, id_eq, SystemGroup.coeff_apply,
    mem_centralizer_singleton_iff, ← commute_iff_eq] at hv
  simp only [SetLike.mem_coe]
  rw [← QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff, ← QuotientGroup.mk'_apply, map_commutatorElement,
    commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute]
  simpa using hv.zpow_right k

namespace Standard

variable {x y : Fin 0 → X} {z : Fin 1 → X} {hnm : 0 < 0 + 1}
  {inj : (Sum.elim (Sum.elim x y) z).Injective} {c : Fin 1 → G} {hc : ∀ j, c j ≠ 1} {d : G}
  {hd : d ≠ 1}

/-- The standard quadratic equation of type (2) with genus `0` and one conjugated coefficient is
the conjugacy equation `z⁻¹ c z d = 1`. -/
theorem toWord_orientableCoeff_zero_one : (orientableCoeff 0 1 hnm x y z inj c hc d hd).toWord =
    (inr (FreeGroup.of (z 0)))⁻¹ * inl (c 0) * inr (FreeGroup.of (z 0)) * inl d := by
  simp [toWord_def]

/-- **The radical of `c^z d = 1`** ([VAR-I], §3, Form 2). Over a CSA group `G` with the big powers
property, the radical of the one-variable standard quadratic equation `z⁻¹ c z d = 1` with
`d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ c b` is the normal closure of the commutators `⁅z b⁻¹, h⁆`, `h ∈ C_G(c)`. -/
theorem Rad_toWord_orientableCoeff_zero_one [Subsingleton X] (hBP : IsBP G) (hG : IsCSA G) {b : G}
    (hb : d⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * c 0 * b) :
    Rad (.id G) {(orientableCoeff 0 1 hnm x y z inj c hc d hd).toWord} =
      normalClosure ((fun h => ⁅inr (FreeGroup.of (z 0)) * inl b⁻¹, inl h⁆) ''
        (centralizer {c 0} : Set G)) := by
  rw [toWord_orientableCoeff_zero_one, Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure hBP hG (hc 0) hb]

end Standard

/-! ### Examples -/

section Examples

open FreeGroup

/-- Over `F(a, t)`, the commutator `⁅z, a⁆` lies in the radical of `z⁻¹ a² z a⁻² = 1`, since `a`
centralizes `a²`. -/
theorem commutator_mem_rad_conj_sq :
    let a : FreeGroup (Fin 2) := of 0
    let z : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit := inr (of ())
    ⁅z, inl a⁆ ∈ Rad (.id _) {z⁻¹ * inl (a ^ 2) * z * inl (a ^ 2)⁻¹} := by
  intro a z
  have hF : IsFullyResiduallyFree (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) := isFullyResiduallyFree_of_isFreeGroup
  rw [Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure hF.isBP hF.isCSA (b := 1) (by decide) (by simp)]
  exact subset_normalClosure ⟨a, mem_centralizer_singleton_iff.2 (pow_mul_comm' _ _).symm,
    by simp [z]⟩

/-- Over `F(a, t)`, the commutator `⁅z, a⁆` is not in the normal closure of `⁅z, a²⁆`: it
survives in `S₃` under `a ↦ (0 1)`, `t ↦ 1`, `z ↦ (1 2)`, which kills `⁅z, a²⁆`. So the
single-relator formula fails for `z⁻¹ a² z a⁻² = 1`, whose coefficient `a²` has the larger
centralizer `⟨a⟩`. -/
theorem commutator_notMem_normalClosure_commutator_sq :
    let a : FreeGroup (Fin 2) := of 0
    let z : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit := inr (of ())
    ⁅z, inl a⁆ ∉ normalClosure {⁅z, (inl (a ^ 2) : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit)⁆} := by
  intro a z
  let f : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit →* Equiv.Perm (Fin 3) :=
    eval (FreeGroup.lift ![Equiv.swap (0 : Fin 3) 1, 1]) fun _ => Equiv.swap 1 2
  have hle : normalClosure {⁅z, inl (a ^ 2)⁆} ≤ f.ker := by
    refine normalClosure_le_normal ?_
    rintro _ rfl
    simp only [SetLike.mem_coe, MonoidHom.mem_ker, map_commutatorElement, f, z, a, eval_inl,
      eval_inr, FreeGroup.lift_apply_of, map_pow, Matrix.cons_val_zero]
    decide
  intro h
  have := hle h
  simp only [MonoidHom.mem_ker, map_commutatorElement, f, z, a, eval_inl, eval_inr,
    FreeGroup.lift_apply_of, Matrix.cons_val_zero] at this
  revert this
  decide

/-- Over `F(a, t)`, the centralizer of `a` is `⟨a⟩`, so the radical of `z⁻¹ a z a⁻¹ = 1` is the
normal closure of the equation. -/
theorem rad_conj_eq_normalClosure :
    let a : FreeGroup (Fin 2) := of 0
    let z : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit := inr (of ())
    Rad (.id _) {z⁻¹ * inl a * z * inl a⁻¹} = normalClosure {z⁻¹ * inl a * z * inl a⁻¹} := by
  intro a _
  have hF : IsFullyResiduallyFree (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) := isFullyResiduallyFree_of_isFreeGroup
  exact Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure_self hF.isBP hF.isCSA (b := 1) (of_ne_one 0)
    (by simp [a]) (IsFreeGroup.centralizer_singleton_eq_zpowers_self (not_isProperPower_of 0)).le ()

/- In `G = ⟨a, b, t | [a, t] = 1⟩`, the extension of the centralizer `⟨a⟩` of `a` in `F(a, b)`,
every commutator `⁅z, h⁆` with `h` centralizing `a` lies in the radical of `z⁻¹ a z a⁻¹ = 1`. -/
private theorem commutator_mem_rad_conj_centralizerExt
    {h : CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1}
    (hh : h ∈ centralizer {CentralizerExt.of (of 0)}) :
    let a : CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1 := CentralizerExt.of (of 0)
    let z : GX (CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1) Unit := inr (of ())
    ⁅z, inl h⁆ ∈ Rad (.id _) {z⁻¹ * inl a * z * inl a⁻¹} := by
  intro a z
  have hK : IsFullyResiduallyFree (CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1) :=
    isFullyResiduallyFree_centralizerExt isFullyResiduallyFree_of_isFreeGroup (of_ne_one 0)
  rw [Rad_conjugacy_eq_normalClosure hK.isBP hK.isCSA (b := 1)
    ((map_ne_one_iff _ CentralizerExt.of_injective).2 (of_ne_one 0)) (by simp [a])]
  exact subset_normalClosure ⟨h, hh, by simp [z]⟩

/-- In `G = ⟨a, b, t | [a, t] = 1⟩`, the extension of the centralizer `⟨a⟩` of `a` in `F(a, b)`,
the new letter `t` centralizes `a`, so `⁅z, t⁆` lies in the radical of `z⁻¹ a z a⁻¹ = 1`. -/
theorem commutator_t_mem_rad_conj_centralizerExt :
    let a : CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1 := CentralizerExt.of (of 0)
    let z : GX (CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1) Unit := inr (of ())
    ⁅z, inl (CentralizerExt.t 0)⁆ ∈ Rad (.id _) {z⁻¹ * inl a * z * inl a⁻¹} :=
  commutator_mem_rad_conj_centralizerExt <| mem_centralizer_singleton_iff.2
    (CentralizerExt.commute_of_t (mem_centralizer_singleton_iff.2 rfl) 0).eq.symm

/-- In `G = ⟨a, b, t | [a, t] = 1⟩`, the commutator `⁅z, a⁆` also lies in the radical of
`z⁻¹ a z a⁻¹ = 1`. -/
theorem commutator_of_mem_rad_conj_centralizerExt :
    let a : CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1 := CentralizerExt.of (of 0)
    let z : GX (CentralizerExt (of 0 : FreeGroup (Fin 2)) 1) Unit := inr (of ())
    ⁅z, inl a⁆ ∈ Rad (.id _) {z⁻¹ * inl a * z * inl a⁻¹} :=
  commutator_mem_rad_conj_centralizerExt (mem_centralizer_singleton_iff.2 rfl)

end Examples

end TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Quadratic
